sec.p:
For the first case, cross-correlation coefficients between two pure Gaussian noise images with Np pixels can be approximated with a Gaussian distribution with zero mean and a variance of 1/Np, where Np is the number of pixels in the image. In cryo-EM particle alignment, a five-dimensional search is performed for each particle, including two translational parameters (x and y) and three orientational parameters (ϕ, θ, ψ). For each particle, Ns correlation coefficients are calculated to find the correct alignment. Ns is dependent on the size of the particle and the resolution limit of the alignment. A larger particle and higher resolution limit require a more finely sampled search space. The parameter set, Θ0 = {x0, y0, ϕ0, θ0, ψ0}, corresponding to the maximum value among Ns correlations, is then used to register the particle’s alignment. We define SNRn using the maximum and standard deviation of Ns correlation coefficients. Based on Supporting Information A,
(1)
SNRn=2log⁡Ns. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{SNR}_{n}=\sqrt{2 \log N_{s}} . \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

For the second case, we follow Henderson’s assumption that the particle is roughly spherical and consists of only randomly positioned carbon atoms (16). Cross-correlations are calculated between a M -frame summed image and 2D projections from the perfect reference. Similarly, SNRs is defined as the ratio between the maximum cross-correlation and the standard deviation of background correlations. Based on Supporting Information B,
(2)
SNRs=4fN0D2∫kCTF(k)2(∑i=1MQ(k,Ni))2dk \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{SNR}_{s}=\sqrt{4 f N_{0} D^{2} \int_{k} \mathrm{CTF}(k)^{2}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{M} Q\left(k, N_{i}\right)\right)^{2} d k} \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Q(k, Ni) is the normalized exposure-weighting transfer function at spatial frequency k for cumulative frame exposure Ni (19), N0 is the exposure per frame, D is the particle diameter, and f is the fraction of electrons being elastically scattered. Assuming a total exposure of 5 e−/Å2 and a single-frame acquisition (16), we derived a simplified form of Eq. 2 by assuming a realistic CTF with multiple oscillations, such that the integral ∫k CTF(k)2dk ≈ 0.5. This leads to
(3)
SNRs=2fNeD. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{SNR}_{s}=\sqrt{2 f N_{e}} D . \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

For two independent Gaussian noise images of Np pixels, the normalized cross-correlation, rn, is zero on average. The distribution of rn when Np is large can be approximated by a Gaussian distribution
(S1)
rn∼N(0,1Np). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r_{n} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(0, \frac{1}{N_{p}}\right) . \tag{S1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

In cryo-EM particle alignment, normalized cross-correlations are computed between a noisy particle image and a set of clean 2D projections generated at sampled orientations, in order to determine the best-matching position (x, y) and orientation. For each particle image, Ns cross-correlations are evaluated, and the alignment is assigned based on the position and orientation corresponding to the maximum cross-correlation. This process is equivalent to drawing the maximal value from Ns Gaussian distributed random variables with zero mean and variance of 1Np\begin{document}$\frac{1}{N_{p}}$\end{document}. The upper bound of the expectation of this maximum is (64)
(S2)
E(rn,max)≤σcc2log⁡Ns=2log⁡NsNp. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbb{E}\left(r_{n, \max }\right) \leq \sigma_{c c} \sqrt{2 \log N_{s}}=\frac{\sqrt{2 \log N_{s}}}{\sqrt{N_{p}}} . \tag{S2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We define the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) of alignment noise as the number of standard deviations (SDs) by which the maximum cross-correlation exceeds the SD of cross-correlations computed across a pure noise image:
(S3)
SNRn=2log⁡NsNp/1/Np=2log⁡Ns. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{SNR}_{n}=\frac{\sqrt{2 \log N_{s}}}{\sqrt{N_{p}}} / \sqrt{1 / N_{p}}=\sqrt{2 \log N_{s}} . \tag{S3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

list-item.p:
Out-of-plane viewing directions:


Nviews≈4π(Δθ)2=4π(2p/D)2=π(Dp)2
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle N_{\mathrm{views}} \approx \frac{4 \pi}{(\Delta \theta)^{2}}=\frac{4 \pi}{(2 p / D)^{2}}=\pi\left(\frac{D}{p}\right)^{2}$$\end{document}

sec.p:
The total number of correlations calculated during a five-dimensional search is then
(S4)
Ns=32π2(Dd)5. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} N_{s}=32 \pi^{2}\left(\frac{D}{d}\right)^{5} . \tag{S4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

We assume that the particle consists of only randomly positioned carbon atoms. The electron atomic scattering factor of carbon can be approximated as a sum of (normally) five Gaussians (65, 66):
(S5)
f(e)(k)=∑i=15aiexp⁡(−bik2), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f^{(e)}(k)=\sum_{i=1}^{5} a_{i} \exp \left(-b_{i} k^{2}\right), \tag{S5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where ai and bi are the fitting parameters up to 6 Å−1 (67), and k is the spatial frequency (Å−1). The differential scattering cross-section is:
(S6)
dσedΩ=|f(e)(θ)|2, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d \sigma_{e}}{d \Omega}=\left|f^{(e)}(\theta)\right|^{2}, \tag{S6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where θ is the scattering angle and
(S7)
k=sin⁡(θ2)λ, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} k=\frac{\sin \left(\frac{\theta}{2}\right)}{\lambda}, \tag{S7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where λ is the electron wavelength. The differential cross-section for a single, isolated atom is related to θ by:
(S8)
dσedΩ=12πsin⁡θdσedθ. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d \sigma_{e}}{d \Omega}=\frac{1}{2 \pi \sin \theta} \frac{d \sigma_{e}}{d \theta} . \tag{S8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

We now integrate to calculate the total scattering cross-section:
(S9)
σe=∫0θmax2πsin⁡θ|f(e)(k)|2dθ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \sigma_{e}=\int_{0}^{\theta_{\max }} 2 \pi \sin \theta\left|f^{(e)}(k)\right|^{2} d \theta \tag{S9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Assuming protein density ρ ≈ 0.8 Da/Å3, the number of carbon atoms equivalent to a spherical protein of diameter D (Å) is (16):
(S10)
Nc=Mw⋅9.1110=43π(D2)3ρ⋅9.1110. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} N_{c}=\frac{M_{w} \cdot 9.1}{110}=\frac{4}{3} \pi\left(\frac{D}{2}\right)^{3} \rho \cdot \frac{9.1}{110} . \tag{S10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The fraction of electrons being elastically scattered by the particle is:
(S11)
f=σe(C)NcD2. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f=\frac{\sigma_{e}(\mathrm{C}) N_{c}}{D^{2}} . \tag{S11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The wave function in Fourier space after lens aberration is (6, 68):
Ψpc(k)=Ψexit(k,z)⋅exp⁡[−iχ(k)]=Ψinc(z)[δ(k)+iσF(k)]⋅exp⁡(−iχ(k)), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \Psi_{\mathrm{pc}}(k) & =\Psi_{\mathrm{exit}}(k, z) \cdot \exp [-i \chi(k)] \\ & =\Psi_{\mathrm{inc}}(z)[\delta(k)+i \sigma F(k)] \cdot \exp (-i \chi(k)),\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
in which the lens aberration function (69) is
(S14)
χ(k)=πΔfλk2−π2Csλ3k4 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \chi(k)=\pi \Delta f \lambda k^{2}-\frac{\pi}{2} C_{s} \lambda^{3} k^{4} \tag{S14} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Here, Δf is the defocus (positive for underfocus) and Cs is the spherical aberration. The contrast transfer function (CTF) is defined as
(S15)
CTF(k)=−sin⁡χ(k). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \text { CTF }(k)=-\sin \chi(k) . \tag{S15} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The Fourier transform of the linearized intensity is (6, 68):
(S16)
I0(k)=|Ψpc(k)|2≈δ(k)+2σF(k)CTF(k). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{0}(k)=\left|\Psi_{\mathrm{pc}}(k)\right|^{2} \approx \delta(k)+2 \sigma F(k) \operatorname{CTF}(k) . \tag{S16} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Given the per-frame per-unit area exposure d24N0\begin{document}$\frac{d^{2}}{4} N_{0}$\end{document} where N0 is the exposure per-frame, the observed noisy image of a single frame is
(S17)
Iinoisy(x)∼Poisson[d24N0F−1[δ(k)+2σF(k)CTF(k)]]≈Ii(x)+ni(x)+DC, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} I_{i}^{\text {noisy }}(x) & \sim \text { Poisson }\left[\frac{d^{2}}{4} N_{0} \mathcal{F}^{-1}[\delta(k)+2 \sigma F(k) \operatorname{CTF}(k)]\right] \\ & \approx I_{i}(x)+n_{i}(x)+\mathrm{DC}, \tag{S17} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
where
(S18)
Ii(x)=d24N0F−1[2σF(k)CTF(k)]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{i}(x)=\frac{d^{2}}{4} N_{0} \mathcal{F}^{-1}[2 \sigma F(k) \operatorname{CTF}(k)] . \tag{S18} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Equivalently, in Fourier space
(S19)
Iinoisy(k)≈Si(k)+ni(k), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{i}^{\text {noisy }}(k) \approx S_{i}(k)+n_{i}(k), \tag{S19} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where
(S20)
Si(k)=d24N02σF(k)CTF(k). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} S_{i}(k)=\frac{d^{2}}{4} N_{0} 2 \sigma F(k) \operatorname{CTF}(k) . \tag{S20} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

We now define an SNR value as the expected value of cross-correlations generated from phase contrast, divided by the SD of the correlations from noise
(S22)
SNRs=CCsσ(CCn). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{SNR}_{s}=\frac{C C_{s}}{\sigma\left(C C_{n}\right)} . \tag{S22} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

For an image summed over M frames, Eq. S19 is updated with an exposure filter Q(k, Ni) calculated from (19). Here, Q(k, Ni) denotes the normalized exposure-weighting transfer function at spatial frequency k for frame dose Ni (Ni = i N0). The unnormalized exposure-dependent weight is
(S23)
q(k,N)=e−N2NE(k), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} q(k, N)=e^{\frac{-N}{2 N_{E}(k)}}, \tag{S23} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
and the normalized filter is
(S24)
Q(k,Ni)=q(k,Ni)∑iq(k,Ni)2. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} Q\left(k, N_{i}\right)=\frac{q\left(k, N_{i}\right)}{\sqrt{\sum_{i} q\left(k, N_{i}\right)^{2}}} . \tag{S24} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The processed image is then
(S25)
Iiproc(k)=Q(k,Ni)[Si(k)+ni(k)]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{i}^{\mathrm{proc}}(k)=Q\left(k, N_{i}\right)\left[S_{i}(k)+n_{i}(k)\right] . \tag{S25} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The summed image:
(S27)
Isum(k)=∑i=1MIiproc(k)=∑i=1MQ(k,Ni)Si(k)+∑i=1MQ(k,Ni)ni(k)=Ssum(k)+nsum(k) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} I_{\mathrm{sum}}(k) & =\sum_{i=1}^{M} I_{i}^{\mathrm{proc}}(k) \\ & =\sum_{i=1}^{M} Q\left(k, N_{i}\right) S_{i}(k)+\sum_{i=1}^{M} Q\left(k, N_{i}\right) n_{i}(k) \\ & =S_{\mathrm{sum}}(k)+n_{\mathrm{sum}}(k) \tag{S27} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
where
(S28)
Ssum(k)=d24N02σF(k)CTF(k)∑i=1MQ(k,Ni). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} S_{\text {sum }}(k)=\frac{d^{2}}{4} N_{0} 2 \sigma F(k) \operatorname{CTF}(k) \sum_{i=1}^{M} Q\left(k, N_{i}\right) . \tag{S28} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Using a perfect reference that is also exposure filtered
(S29)
R(k)=Ssum∗(k)σ02, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} R(k)=\frac{S_{\mathrm{sum}}^{*}(k)}{\sigma_{0}^{2}}, \tag{S29} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
the expected CC from the signal is
(S30)
CCs=∫kSsum(k)⋅Ssum∗(k)σ02dk=1σ02∫k|Ssum(k)|2dk \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C C_{s}=\int_{k} \frac{S_{\mathrm{sum}}(k) \cdot S_{\mathrm{sum}}^{*}(k)}{\sigma_{0}^{2}} d k=\frac{1}{\sigma_{0}^{2}} \int_{k}\left|S_{\mathrm{sum}}(k)\right|^{2} d k \tag{S30} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

For noise, the mean CC is zero and the variance is:
(S31)
Var(CCn)=∫k|Ssum(k)σ02|2⋅σ02dk=1σ02∫k|Ssum(k)|2dk \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \operatorname{Var}\left(C C_{n}\right)=\int_{k}\left|\frac{S_{\mathrm{sum}}(k)}{\sigma_{0}^{2}}\right|^{2} \cdot \sigma_{0}^{2} d k=\frac{1}{\sigma_{0}^{2}} \int_{k}\left|S_{\mathrm{sum}}(k)\right|^{2} d k \tag{S31} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

We further add a correction for inelastic scattering (assuming the use of an energy filter to remove inelastically scattered electrons) using:
(S33)
Neff=Ne⋅exp⁡(−tΛin), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} N_{\mathrm{eff}}=N_{e} \cdot \exp \left(\frac{-t}{\Lambda_{\mathrm{in}}}\right), \tag{S33} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where t is the sample thickness and Λin is the inelastic scattering mean free path of the solution. The inelastic mean free path depends on the incident electron energy and is related to the scattering cross-section as:
(S34)
Λin=MwρNAσin,sol, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Lambda_{\text {in }}=\frac{M_{w}}{\rho N_{A} \sigma_{\text {in,sol }}}, \tag{S34} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where σin,sol is the weighted inelastic scattering cross-section calculated by individual atom’s inelastic scattering cross-sections (71, 72):
(S35)
σin=1.5⋅10−6Z12β2ln⁡β2(U0+mc2)10[nm2] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \sigma_{\mathrm{in}}=\frac{1.5 \cdot 10^{-6} Z^{\frac{1}{2}}}{\beta^{2}} \ln \frac{\beta^{2}\left(U_{0}+m c^{2}\right)}{10} \quad\left[\mathrm{~nm}^{2}\right] \tag{S35} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Z is atomic number, β the ratio between velocity of the electron and light, U0 the incident electron energy and mc2 the rest energy of the electron.

For vitreous ice,
(S36)
σin, sol=2σin,H+1σin,O. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \sigma_{\text {in, sol }}=2 \sigma_{\text {in, } \mathrm{H}}+1 \sigma_{\text {in, } \mathrm{O}} . \tag{S36} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Based on (36), Eq. S15 can be extended to
(S37)
CTF(λ,k,Δf,Cs,Δϕ,w)=−sin⁡[χ(λ,|k|,Δf,Cs,Δϕ,w)] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \operatorname{CTF}\left(\lambda, k, \Delta f, C_{s}, \Delta \phi, w\right)=-\sin \left[\chi\left(\lambda,|k|, \Delta f, C_{s}, \Delta \phi, w\right)\right] \tag{S37} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where
(S38)
χ(λ,k,Δf,Cs,Δϕ,w)=πλk2(Δf−12λ2k2Cs)+Δϕ+arctan⁡(w1−w2). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \chi\left(\lambda, k, \Delta f, C_{s}, \Delta \phi, w\right)=\pi \lambda k^{2}\left(\Delta f-\frac{1}{2} \lambda^{2} k^{2} C_{s}\right)+\Delta \phi+\arctan \left(\frac{w}{\sqrt{1-w^{2}}}\right) . \tag{S38} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Defocus has variation due to the particle “depth” D. We further account for this in the CTF based on (6, 37). For each depth slice z, the updated phase shift (Eq. S38) is
(S39)
χ′(z)=πλk2(Δf+z−12λ2k2Cs)+Δϕ+arctan⁡(w1−w2)=χ(λ,k,Δf,Cs,Δϕ,w)+πλk2z \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \chi^{\prime}(z) & =\pi \lambda k^{2}\left(\Delta f+z-\frac{1}{2} \lambda^{2} k^{2} C_{s}\right)+\Delta \phi+\arctan \left(\frac{w}{\sqrt{1-w^{2}}}\right) \\ & =\chi\left(\lambda, k, \Delta f, C_{s}, \Delta \phi, w\right)+\pi \lambda k^{2} z \tag{S39} \end{align*}$$\end{document}

Hence, the depth-averaged CTF is
(S40)
CTFD(λ,k,Δf,Cs,Δϕ,w)=−1D∫−D/2D/2sin⁡[χ′(z)]dz=−1D∫−D/2D/2sin⁡χ(λ,k,Δf,Cs,Δϕ,w)cos⁡(πλk2z)+cos⁡χ(λ,k,Δf,Cs,Δϕ,w)sin⁡(πλk2z)dz=−sin⁡χ(λ,k,Δf,Cs,Δϕ,w)D∫−D/2D/2cos⁡(πλk2z)dz−cos⁡χ(λ,k,Δf,Cs,Δϕ,w)D∫−D/2D/2sin⁡(πλk2z)dz=−sin⁡χ(λ,k,Δf,Cs,Δϕ,w)⋅sin⁡(πλk2D/2)πλk2D/2+0=−sin⁡χ(λ,k,Δf,Cs,Δϕ,w)⋅sinc[πλk2D2] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \operatorname{CTF}_{D}\left(\lambda, k, \Delta f, C_{s}, \Delta \phi, w\right) & =-\frac{1}{D} \int_{-D / 2}^{D / 2} \sin \left[\chi^{\prime}(z)\right] d z \\ & =-\frac{1}{D} \int_{-D / 2}^{D / 2} \sin \chi\left(\lambda, k, \Delta f, C_{s}, \Delta \phi, w\right) \cos \left(\pi \lambda k^{2} z\right)+\cos \chi\left(\lambda, k, \Delta f, C_{s}, \Delta \phi, w\right) \sin \left(\pi \lambda k^{2} z\right) d z \\ & =-\frac{\sin \chi\left(\lambda, k, \Delta f, C_{s}, \Delta \phi, w\right)}{D} \int_{-D / 2}^{D / 2} \cos \left(\pi \lambda k^{2} z\right) d z-\frac{\cos \chi\left(\lambda, k, \Delta f, C_{s}, \Delta \phi, w\right)}{D} \int_{-D / 2}^{D / 2} \sin \left(\pi \lambda k^{2} z\right) d z \\ & =-\sin \chi\left(\lambda, k, \Delta f, C_{s}, \Delta \phi, w\right) \cdot \frac{\sin \left(\pi \lambda k^{2} D / 2\right)}{\pi \lambda k^{2} D / 2}+0 \\ & =-\sin \chi\left(\lambda, k, \Delta f, C_{s}, \Delta \phi, w\right) \cdot \operatorname{sinc}\left[\frac{\pi \lambda k^{2} D}{2}\right] \tag{S40} \end{align*}$$\end{document}

Based on the fact that electron diffraction spots fade 1.2-1.8× slower at liquid helium compared to using liquid nitrogen (24), the exposure filter function can be updated
(S41)
q(k,N)=exp⁡(−N2×1.8NE(k))=exp⁡(−N3.6NE(k)). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} q(k, N)=\exp \left(-\frac{N}{2 \times 1.8 N_{E}(k)}\right)=\exp \left(-\frac{N}{3.6 N_{E}(k)}\right) . \tag{S41} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
